Great. Solution: compute prefix sums, count non-strict inversions via BIT with coordinate compression.

Complexity O(n log n). n up to 1e5, fine.

Implementation:

```python
class Solution:
    def countRatioSubarrays(self, nums: list[int], a: int, b: int) -> int:
        n = len(nums)
        P = [0] * (n + 1)
        for i, v in enumerate(nums):
            P[i+1] = P[i] + (b if v % 2 == 0 else -a)
        # coordinate compress
        sorted_vals = sorted(set(P))
        comp = {v: i+1 for i, v in enumerate(sorted_vals)}
        size = len(sorted_vals)
        bit = [0] * (size + 2)
        def update(i):
            while i <= size:
                bit[i] += 1
                i += i & (-i)
        def query(i):  # sum of [1..i]
            s = 0
            while i > 0:
                s += bit[i]
                i -= i & (-i)
            return s
        ans = 0
        # process from right to left: for each j, count i < j with P[i] >= P[j]
        # equivalently iterate j from n down to 0, query count of values >= P[j] already inserted (those are i > j... hmm)
```

Better: iterate j from 0..n, and for each j count previously inserted i (i < j) with P[i] >= P[j]. Insert P[j] after query.

Count of inserted values >= P[j] = total_inserted - query(comp[P[j]] - 1).

```python
        ans = 0
        inserted = 0
        for j in range(n + 1):
            c = comp[P[j]]
            # number of previous with value >= P[j]
            ans += inserted - query(c - 1)
            update(c)
            inserted += 1
        return ans
```

